WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.647
Now we will look at the safer pattern when we want to preserve the left-side population.

00:00:05.647 --> 00:00:12.307
The first condition in ON matches patients to lab results by patient_id.

00:00:12.307 --> 00:00:18.042
The second condition says that only Glucose rows are eligible to match.

00:00:18.042 --> 00:00:21.507
Run the query and check the patient IDs.

00:00:21.507 --> 00:00:27.352
Every patient should still appear because the LEFT JOIN preserves the patient table.

00:00:27.352 --> 00:00:29.982
Then inspect the lab columns.

00:00:29.982 --> 00:00:33.952
Patients with a Glucose test will show test information.

00:00:33.952 --> 00:00:39.242
Patients without one will remain, but their lab fields will be NULL.

00:00:39.242 --> 00:00:39.992
This is a very useful real-world pattern.

00:00:39.992 --> 00:00:48.787
Suppose a company wants every employee listed, along with their most recent safety training if the training

00:00:48.787 --> 00:00:51.312
was completed this year.

00:00:51.312 --> 00:00:59.007
Or a university wants every student listed, along with a tutoring visit if the visit occurred this semester.

00:00:59.007 --> 00:01:06.977
The date or category restriction often belongs in the matching logic when the population must remain complete.

00:01:06.977 --> 00:01:12.187
Before you run the query, predict which rows should have non-null values.

00:01:12.187 --> 00:01:14.977
Then compare your prediction to the result.

00:01:14.977 --> 00:01:18.640
That habit turns SQL from trial-and-error into reasoning.
